<Project>

  <!-- Windows Smart App Control blocks unsigned binaries by file HASH, and a
       .NET SDK build is deterministic by default: the same sources compile to
       the same bytes forever. Those two facts together make a SAC block
       permanent. Once SAC has ruled on OrdoSort.dll's hash, every subsequent
       build reproduces that exact hash, so rebuilding — the developer's first
       instinct, and the thing that fixes almost everything else — provably
       cannot clear it. Measured on 2026-08-25 with SAC in Enforced mode
       (HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\CI\Policy
       VerifiedAndReputablePolicyState = 1):

         deterministic build   -> OrdoSort.dll SHA256 806A4F6F...  process dies
                                  at startup, exit 0xE0434352, CodeIntegrity
                                  event 3077 "did not meet the Enterprise
                                  signing level requirements"
         Deterministic=false   -> OrdoSort.dll SHA256 94BF8C38...  app runs,
                                  zero block events

       Note what is NOT blocked: OrdoSort.exe. That file is the SDK's apphost
       stub, it starts fine, and it dies loading the managed assembly beside
       it. This matters because it rules out the two fixes that look obvious.
       Relaunching through `dotnet OrdoSort.dll` to bypass the stub does not
       help — the CodeIntegrity log shows dotnet.exe blocked loading the very
       same dll — and rebuilding to "refresh" the stub does not help either,
       since the stub's bytes are SDK template + app name + version resource
       and do not change when you edit code.

       So the one lever that works is the assembly's hash, and the only
       supported way to move it is to turn determinism off. That is scoped to
       Debug deliberately: Release builds are what publish.bat ships, and
       reproducible release bytes are worth more than local convenience.
       Release binaries need a real code-signing certificate, not this.

       This lives in Directory.Build.TARGETS rather than Directory.Build.props
       because $(Configuration) is not yet defined when props is imported —
       measured, not assumed: a plain `dotnet build` evaluates $(Configuration)
       as "" in Directory.Build.props and as "Debug" in Directory.Build.targets,
       so the same condition written in props would silently never fire for the
       exact builds it exists to fix. NonDeterministicDebugBuildTests guards
       both halves of this. -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug'">
    <Deterministic>false</Deterministic>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
